# inputs, and they are all invented or published-example values. None is live.
#
# They CANNOT be handled by an allowlist in .gitleaks.toml. An allowlist
# matches by value, and the value is exactly what those tests assert gets
# reported — allowlisting the invented AKIA-shaped key on line 189 of test_gitleaks_config.py would make
# `test_aws_example_key_excused_but_a_real_shaped_key_is_not` pass while
# asserting nothing. The allowlist would silently disarm the test written to
# catch a silently disarmed allowlist.
#
# ===========================================================================
# WHY A FINGERPRINT IS THE RIGHT GRANULARITY
# ===========================================================================
# A fingerprint is `commit:file:rule:startline`. It names ONE finding, of ONE
# rule, at ONE line, in ONE commit. It cannot widen:
#
#   * a new credential elsewhere in the same file      -> different line   -> REPORTED
#   * a different rule firing on the same line         -> different rule   -> REPORTED
#   * one of these fixtures changed or moved           -> different commit -> REPORTED
#   * a real credential committed anywhere else        -> no entry         -> REPORTED
#
# Compare the two mechanisms this replaced, both of which failed in the
# widening direction:
#   - `allowlist.paths` is a FILE-LEVEL SKIP applied before the file is read,
#     so `condition = "AND"` never runs. It exempted eight whole files.
#   - splitting each literal into adjacent string fragments left the secret in
#     the text and merely moved where the rule anchored; the finding count went
#     UP, from 13 to 19, and it broke five tests.
#
# Fingerprints pin to a commit, so this file goes stale the moment those files
# change. That is the intended failure direction: stale entries stop matching
# and the findings come back loud. Regenerate deliberately, never in bulk:
#
#     gitleaks detect --source . --config .gitleaks.toml \
#       --report-format json --report-path /tmp/gl.json
#
# and add only the entries you have personally read and confirmed are test
# inputs. An entry added without reading the line it silences is how a real
# credential gets in here.
#
